I found an exact factor-free formula for \(u_2(N)\) on one full-\(S_3\) curve. It reduces the question to distinguishing **four representations by a fixed quadratic form from zero or eight**. The direct algorithm still takes \(O(\sqrt N)\) iterations. I also found a distinction that matters for recovery: an evaluator that accepts \(N^2\) can reveal \(p+q\bmod\ell\), even though one answer at \(N\) has only \(\ell\) possible values.

## C1. What reciprocity gives

**Proved.** Write \(h(g)=\det(1-g)=1+\det g-\operatorname{tr}g\). For distinct good primes \(p,q\ne\ell\), the Euler factors give
\[
u_\ell(pq)=h(g_p)h(g_q)
=(1+p)(1+q)-(1+q)a_p-(1+p)a_q+a_pa_q\pmod\ell.
\]
A fixed abelian character of a Galois extension of \(\mathbb Q\) gives a Dirichlet character. Its **product** at the two hidden primes is available as \(\chi(N)\). The Weil pairing gives the example \(\det(g_p)\det(g_q)=N\bmod\ell\).

There are two distinct losses of information.

- **Combining the primes already loses information in an abelian group.** Choose any \(g\) with \(h(g)\ne0\). The pairs \((1,g^2)\) and \((g,g)\) have the same product. Their answers are \(0\) and \(h(g)^2\). Thus even an abelian torsion image would not, by itself, supply a Jacobi-style rule. The exception \(h\equiv0\) includes curves with rational \(\ell\)-torsion.
- **For full image, even separate abelian data at each prime are insufficient.** For odd \(\ell\), \(I\) and \(-I\) have the same abelianization and give \(h=0,4\). For \(S_3\) at \(\ell=2\), identity and a 3-cycle have the same sign and give \(h=0,1\). Chebotarev realizes these alternatives while fixing any prescribed finite collection of compatible abelian data, by taking the compositum of the fields. This rules out fixed congruence recipes. The computational power of arbitrary operations on the integer \(N\) remains open. [Repo Proposition NA](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/tracedata_results.md:346)

**Known.** Symmetric powers, tensor products and Rankin–Selberg coefficients can encode more information. For example, \(\operatorname{tr}(\mathrm{Sym}^2g_r)=a_r^2-r\). At squarefree index \(pq\), an Euler-product coefficient still multiplies its two local coefficients. One needs an independent way to evaluate that new coefficient. Similarly, quadratic base change can make an \(S_3\) representation abelian over the quadratic field. Its characters then take **prime ideals** as input. For a split CM prime, the count is \(\operatorname{Norm}(1-\pi_{\mathfrak p})\). Evaluating a Hecke character on \((N)\) combines the conjugate ideals and loses the separate shifted norms. Inert CM primes do give the useful special value \(r+1\) when \(a_r=0\). [Prime-index Frobenius algorithms](https://arxiv.org/abs/1009.5388)

## C2. Exhausting the small cases

**Proved, \(\ell=2\).** For the 2-division cubic, identity, transposition and 3-cycle give respectively \(3,1,0\) roots over the prime field. Only a 3-cycle gives odd group order. Thus \(u_2=1\) exactly when **both cubics are irreducible**, and \(u_2=a_N\bmod2\). Of the 36 ordered pairs in \(S_3^2\), four give 1. A negative discriminant Jacobi *value* forces 0. Even knowing both the Jacobi value and the number of cubic roots modulo \(N\) leaves ambiguity: a 3-cycle paired with identity and two 3-cycles both give Jacobi \(+1\) and zero roots modulo \(N\).

**Proved, \(\ell=3\).** Exhausting all \(48^2\) ordered matrix pairs gives these counts. All three answers occur in either public determinant class.

| \(N\bmod3\) | \(u_3=0\) | \(u_3=1\) | \(u_3=2\) |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| 1 | 783 | 189 | 180 |
| 2 | 792 | 180 | 180 |

The pairs \((I,I)\) and \((-I,-I)\) even agree on \(a_N\bmod3\) and on the action on all four cyclic 3-isogeny kernels. Their answers are 0 and 1. On the promise \(p,q\equiv-1\bmod3\), there *is* a simplification: \(u_3=a_N\bmod3\). I also enumerated all determinant-surjective subgroups up to conjugacy. At \(\ell=3\), three have identically zero \(h\), three have nonconstant \(h\) determined by the **local** abelianization, and three require nonabelian local data. At \(\ell=2\) those counts are \(2,1,1\). The experiment’s curve has full image at both primes. [Enumeration and image witnesses](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/inp_c/coef_groups.py)

**Does the decision factor \(N\)?** For an exact algorithm inside Theorem PT’s pure arithmetic model, yes on a positive fraction of the iid full-image population. The split-rate lower bounds are \(1/18\) for \(u_2\) and \(81/512\) for \(u_3\) in the limiting class law. For an unrestricted algorithm, no such reduction is known. In particular, a reduction using only the one bit at this \(N\) can try both answers and verify any proposed factor. A reduction making genuinely different oracle queries is a separate question. [Propositions IL and PT corollaries](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/tracedata_results.md:327)

## C3. What the recent claims contribute

I treated the supplied manuscripts’ headline claims as **unverified**. Their mechanisms have the following implications for this target.

| Work | Handle on the coefficient |
|---|---|
| Cogentic deterministic \(L[3/4]\) | Biquadratic cocycles and Walsh–Hadamard positivity force quadratic nonresidues. The splitting subroutine takes a common multiple of \(r-1\) for \(r\mid N\). Its short-generator and Gauss-cycle machinery works on constructed ideals. The missing input for the example below is an ideal of norm \(N\) with the required choice above each prime. [Manuscript](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/cogentic/deterministic_factoring_l34.txt) |
| Cogentic \(L[1/3]\) | Smooth relations, square certification and Dixon extraction produce a factor. This would give \(u_\ell\) and \(a_N\) by factoring first at the claimed cost. The certification step supplies no local torsion trace for the input divisors. [Manuscript](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/cogentic/integer_factorization.txt) |
| alpo.ge CM derivatives | The closest positive example. A derivative computed blind modulo \(N\) can vanish at an inert factor. The guaranteed divisibility starts at derivative order \(a\ge p\), and CM inertness itself is quadratic. A polylogarithmic way to jump to that derivative would be a substantive discovery. [Lean-mechanism audit](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/multcopp_results.md:30) |
| OpenAI FFT | The stated bound speeds a full transform on an explicit vector, or a full polynomial product. It supplies no random-access procedure for one coefficient at a binary index \(N\). [Manuscript](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/openai_fft/paper.txt) |
| Masullo GRH | Even effective Chebotarev can locate auxiliary primes with chosen behavior. It supplies no operation identifying the classes of the two divisors of this particular input. The repo also records an incomplete zero-location argument. [Audit](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/masullo_results.md:29) |

## C4. Constructions and experiment

**Proved — a native parity formula.** Take \(E:y^2=x^3-x+1\). Its cubic splitting field is the Hilbert class field of \(\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-23})\), whose class group has order three. Put
\[
R(N)=\#\{(s,t)\in\mathbb Z^2:s^2+st+6t^2=N\}.
\]
For distinct primes \(p,q\nmid46\),
\[
\boxed{\quad u_2(pq)=1\iff R(pq)=4.\quad}
\]
An inert factor gives no ideal of norm \(pq\). If both split, there are four choices of prime ideals, one above each rational prime. If both primes split principally, all four choices are principal. If exactly one does, none are. If both split nonprincipally, exactly two are principal because the class group is \(C_3\). Each principal ideal contributes two generators. This last case is precisely two 3-cycles. Equivalently, \(u_2(N)\) is the \(N\)-th coefficient of \(\eta(z)\eta(23z)\) modulo 2 on these semiprimes. Scanning \(23t^2\le4N\) and testing whether \(4N-23t^2\) is square gives the factor-free algorithm. All three evaluations agreed on **1,891** semiprimes with primes at most 311, including 231 positive cases. [Zagier, Proposition 14](https://www.its.caltech.edu/~matilde/Zagier123ModularForms.pdf) · [Independent check](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/inp_c/coef_theta_check.py)

**Proved — one tempting acceleration fails.** No fixed binary finite automaton extracts the full sequence \(u_2(n)\) for this curve. Its odd support has density zero: at a density-\(2/3\) set of primes \(r\), valuation exactly one forces an even coefficient by multiplicativity. The finite-prime sieve densities therefore tend to zero. Yet the support contains \(\gg X/\log X\) primes up to \(X\). For a finite automaton reading random binary digits, acceptance probability tending to zero decays exponentially in the digit length, since every reachable accepting state is transient. These bounds conflict. By Christol’s theorem this also excludes an algebraic ordinary generating series over \(\mathbb F_2(z)\). The proof uses all indices and leaves the semiprime promise open. [Christol’s theorem](https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.10977)

**Empirical — preregistered sampling.** I used 800 balanced semiprimes at each of 40, 60 and 80 bits, half held out. From \(N\) alone I sampled six random multiples of \((0,1)\), removed small-order components with a public scalar, tested for certified \(\ell\)-torsion, and took Jacobi readouts of the resulting \(x+1\). These samples cover a cyclic subgroup. PARI point counts using the hidden factors supplied ground truth. Proper gcds factored **55/800, 0/800, 0/800** inputs respectively. On the remaining 1,175 held-out inputs, there were no torsion witnesses.

| Target | Mean log-score gain over congruence/Jacobi baseline | Paired 99% interval | Accuracy, baseline → sampler |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| \(u_2\) | +0.0011 | [−0.0027, +0.0047] | 89.1% → 89.1% |
| \(u_3\) | −0.0135 | [−0.0236, −0.0034] | 66.6% → 66.6% |

Neither estimator met the preregistered criterion. EXT’s auxiliary algebra gives a different operation: the **known** \(M\) kills a selected component with constant probability. Lifting to \(\mathbb Z/N^k\) gives the count \(N^{k-1}M\), and a rational isogeny preserves both local counts. Neither operation supplies the initial scalar. [Preregistration](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/inp_c/coef_sampling_prereg.txt) · [Sampler](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/inp_c/coef_sampling.py) · [Results](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/inp_c/coef_sampling_result.json) · [Theorem EXT](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/tracedata_results.md:467)

## C5. What would change the verdict

**Proved.** The Dirichlet coefficient at a prime square satisfies \(u(r^2)=(1+r)u(r)\) before reduction. Hence an **all-index** evaluator, whenever \(u_\ell(N)\ne0\), gives
\[
p+q\equiv u_\ell(N^2)/u_\ell(N)-1-N\pmod\ell.
\]
This offers a direct check on any proposed coefficient algorithm. Even a semiprime-only evaluator suffices for factoring if it works uniformly in polynomial time for primes \(\ell\le C\log N\) with sufficiently large constant \(C\). Their product exceeds \(16N\), Hasse gives \(M<16N\), and CRT followed by EXT recovers a factor.

**Open.** The sharpest first test is to decide \(R(N)=4\) in \(\operatorname{poly}(\log N)\) time on good semiprimes with \(\left(\frac{-23}{N}\right)=+1\). The curve, discriminant, class group, and three possible representation counts are all fixed. The unknown operation is extracting that multiplicity at one large integer.

**Speculative.** Conditional on classical polynomial-time factoring existing, I assign about **3%** to a native evaluator of these coefficients providing the first such algorithm. The quadratic-form identity gives a concrete reason to pursue the parity case. The larger jump is from that one bit to a method uniform across enough \(\ell\) to recover \(M\).